//? Title: Variables and Initialisers: Keeping the Signal Alive Through Depth
//? Subtitle: What a variable is in an eager library, why Glorot, He and orthogonal initialisers have the scales they do, the mean-field theory of signals and gradients in deep networks, and what goes wrong when the scale is off, using datamunge's nn::init::variance_scaling and nn::init::signal_propagation
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
In an eager library a variable is a tensor updated in place, and an initialiser is a function that draws its first value. Most of what matters is in the
initialiser's scale. If weights have variance \(s/n_{\text{in}}\), each layer multiplies the variance of the signal by \(s\) times the activation's second-moment factor. Over \(L\)
layers that factor is raised to the power \(L\), so the signal vanishes or explodes unless it equals one. This report derives the Glorot, He and LeCun scales from that
requirement, along with the uniform and truncated-normal corrections. It checks the mean-field recursion for activations and gradients against 50-layer networks: tanh agrees to within 10%, and ReLU
to within the finite-width fluctuations. It also checks the Fuss–Catalan law for the singular values of a deep linear network's Jacobian (mean \(\sigma^4=L+1\)), against which
orthogonal weights are an exact isometry. On the digits data, a 10-hidden-layer ReLU network trained by gradient descent fits the training set with He or orthogonal weights. It
stays near chance for about 160 steps with Glorot weights, and never leaves chance with \(\mathcal N(0,0.01^2)\) weights, whose gradients are \(10^{-15}\). The library gained
`nn::init::variance_scaling`, `nn::init::gain`, `nn::init::truncated_normal_std` and `nn::init::signal_propagation`.
)

\toc

> The problem

TensorFlow 1 variables are graph nodes that own a buffer, start uninitialised, persist across session runs and are set by running an initialiser subgraph
(Abadi et al., 2016). In `datamunge::nn`, as in PyTorch, a variable is an ordinary tensor that is updated in place, usually held in an `nn::ParameterStore`. An initialiser is a function
from a shape to a tensor. The mechanics are therefore simple. The substance is in the numbers an initialiser draws. A network that starts with the wrong scale may train slowly,
erratically or not at all, however good the optimiser.

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.1_tensors_graphs_sessions/variables_initializers.cpp`. That example showed a variable's lifecycle,
wrote the common initialisers from random ops and checked their standard deviations. Those checks are kept here, and the report adds the theory behind the scales and its
consequences in deep networks.

> The method

>> Variables

A variable is a tensor handle. Copying the handle aliases it, and in-place methods (`add_`, `copy_`, `fill_`) write through to the shared storage, so every alias sees the update.
Each write increments the storage's version counter, so autograd can detect a variable changed after it was saved for a backward pass. Updates to a parameter that requires gradients
are made under `NoGradGuard`, outside the tape. `ParameterStore::clone()` makes an independent deep copy, the counterpart of a second TensorFlow session with its own variables. A gradient
with respect to a variable is an ordinary `nn::grad` call.

>> One layer: the variance of a sum

Let a dense layer compute \(z_j=\sum_{i=1}^{n_{\text{in}}}w_{ij}h_i\) with independent zero-mean weights of variance \(v\), independent of the inputs. Then

$$
\operatorname{Var}(z_j)=n_{\text{in}}\,v\,\mathbb E[h_i^2].
$$
\alttext(The variance of z j equals n in times v times the expected value of h i squared.)

If \(h=\phi(z')\) for the previous layer's pre-activation \(z'\sim\mathcal N(0,q)\), then \(\mathbb E[h^2]=\mathbb E[\phi(\sqrt q\,Z)^2]\). For the identity it is \(q\). For ReLU, half of a symmetric distribution survives, so it is \(q/2\).
Keeping \(\operatorname{Var}(z)=q\) therefore needs \(n_{\text{in}}v=1\) for linear layers (LeCun et al., 1998) and \(n_{\text{in}}v=2\) for ReLU (He et al., 2015). In the backward pass the
gradient with respect to \(h_i\) is \(\sum_jw_{ij}\,\partial\ell/\partial z_j\), a sum over \(n_{\text{out}}\) terms, so gradients keep their scale when \(n_{\text{out}}v\) equals the same constant. Glorot and Bengio (2010)
compromise with \(v=2/(n_{\text{in}}+n_{\text{out}})\). All of these are \(v=s/n\), with \(n\) the fan-in, the fan-out or their mean and \(s\) the square of a *gain* (\(\sqrt2\) for ReLU). That is what
`nn::init::variance_scaling(s, mode, distribution)` draws, and `nn::init::gain` gives the usual gains.

>> Matching the variance with bounded distributions

A uniform \(U(-a,a)\) has variance \(a^2/3\), so \(a=\sqrt{3v}\). A normal truncated at two standard deviations is narrower than the normal it came from. For \(Z\sim\mathcal N(0,1)\) conditioned on \(\lvert Z\rvert\le b\),
integrating \(z^2\varphi(z)\) by parts with \(\varphi'(z)=-z\varphi(z)\) gives

$$
\operatorname{Var}(Z\mid\lvert Z\rvert\le b)=1-\frac{2b\,\varphi(b)}{2\Phi(b)-1},
$$
\alttext(The variance of Z given absolute Z at most b equals 1 minus 2 b phi of b over 2 Phi of b minus 1.)

whose square root is 0.87962566 for \(b=2\). Keras and `variance_scaling` divide the truncated draw by this factor so that its variance is exactly \(v\). The library's
`truncated_normal(stddev)` initialiser does not, so its actual standard deviation is \(0.88\times\)`stddev`.

>> Depth: the mean-field recursion

Through \(L\) layers of width \(n\) with \(v=s/n\), write \(q_l\) for the variance of the layer-\(l\) pre-activations. As \(n\to\infty\) the pre-activations become Gaussian, and the one-layer result becomes a deterministic
map (Poole et al., 2016):

$$
q_{l+1}=s\,\mathbb E\bigl[\phi(\sqrt{q_l}\,Z)^2\bigr],\qquad
\frac{\operatorname{Var}(g_l)}{\operatorname{Var}(g_{l+1})}=\chi_l=s\,\mathbb E\bigl[\phi'(\sqrt{q_l}\,Z)^2\bigr],
$$
\alttext(q l plus 1 equals s times the expected value of phi of root q l times Z, squared. The ratio of gradient variances at layers l and l plus 1 equals chi l, which is s times the expected value of phi prime of root q l times Z, squared.)

where \(g_l\) is the gradient with respect to \(z_l\). For ReLU both factors are \(s/2\), so activations and gradients scale like \((s/2)^{l/2}\) in RMS: they stay level only at the He value \(s=2\), and
a 10% error in \(s\) compounds to a factor \(1.1^{25}\approx11\) over 50 layers. For tanh the map has a stable fixed point \(q^\ast>0\) when \(s>1\), so the *forward* signal settles to a constant
for any such \(s\). The gradient factor at the fixed point, \(\chi^\ast\), decides whether gradients grow (\(\chi^\ast>1\), the chaotic phase) or shrink (\(\chi^\ast<1\), the ordered phase)
(Schoenholz et al., 2017). The forward signal alone cannot reveal which.

>> Beyond the variance: the singular values of the Jacobian

The mean field tracks the average squared norm. The network's input–output Jacobian also matters, because its singular values say how differently directions are stretched. For a deep linear
network \(J=W_1\cdots W_L\) with Gaussian \(W_l\) of variance \(1/n\), the squared singular values of \(J\) follow the Fuss–Catalan distribution as \(n\to\infty\)
(Penson and Życzkowski, 2011). Its moments are \(\frac{1}{Lk+1}\binom{(L+1)k}{k}\), so the mean of \(\sigma^2\) is 1, the mean of \(\sigma^4\) is \(L+1\), and the support ends at
\(\sigma^2=(L+1)^{L+1}/L^L\approx e(L+1)\). On average the norm is preserved, but the spread grows with depth: a few directions are stretched by a factor of order \(\sqrt L\), and most
are crushed. Orthogonal \(W_l\) give \(J^\top J=I\) exactly, so every singular value is 1 at any depth, which is *dynamical isometry* (Saxe et al., 2014; Pennington et al., 2017).
`nn::init::orthogonal` draws such matrices uniformly by the sign-corrected QR method (Mezzadri, 2007).

> Simple examples

>> A variable's life, and the initialisers' statistics

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
namespace init = dm::nn::init;
using nn::Tensor;

int main() {
  // A variable's life: no value, a value, in-place updates seen through every alias, an independent clone.
  std::print("| step | code | w | alias of w |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  Tensor w;
  std::string why;
  try { (void)w.to_vector<float>(); } catch (const std::exception& e) { why = e.what(); }
  std::print("| declared | `Tensor w;` | undefined; reading throws \"{}\" | |\n", why);
  w = nn::tensor({{1, 2}, {3, 4}});
  const Tensor alias = w;
  std::print("| assigned | `w = tensor({{{{1, 2}}, {{3, 4}}}})` | {} | {} |\n", w.values_str(), alias.values_str());
  w.add_(nn::full({2, 2}, 1.0));
  std::print("| AssignAdd | `w.add_(full({{2, 2}}, 1))` | {} | {} |\n", w.values_str(), alias.values_str());
  w.copy_(w * 10.0);
  std::print("| Assign | `w.copy_(w * 10)` | {} | {} |\n", w.values_str(), alias.values_str());
  w.copy_(nn::concat({nn::slice_axis(w, 0, 0, 1), nn::tensor({{-1, -2}})}, 0));
  std::print("| row update | `w.copy_(concat({{row 0, {{{{-1, -2}}}}}}, 0))` | {} | {} |\n", w.values_str(), alias.values_str());
  nn::ParameterStore store(1);
  store.set("w", w);
  nn::ParameterStore other = store.clone();
  {
    nn::NoGradGuard no_grad;
    other.at("w").add_(nn::full({2, 2}, 100.0));
  }
  std::print("| clone, then add 100 to the clone | `store.clone()` | {} | clone: {} |\n", store.at("w").values_str(), other.at("w").values_str());
  Tensor v = nn::tensor({1, 2, 3}).requires_grad_();
  {
    nn::NoGradGuard no_grad;
    v.add_(nn::tensor({10, 10, 10}));
  }
  std::print("| gradient with respect to a variable | `grad(sum(v²), {{v}})`, v = {} | {} | |\n", v.values_str(), nn::grad(nn::sum(nn::square(v)), {v})[0].values_str());
  std::vector<std::vector<float>> seeded;
  for (int run = 0; run < 2; ++run) {
    nn::Generator g(123);
    seeded.push_back(nn::normal({3}, 0.0, 1.0, g).to_vector<float>());
  }
  std::print("| two runs seeded with 123 | `normal({{3}}, 0, 1, Generator(123))` | [{:.5f}, {:.5f}, {:.5f}] | [{:.5f}, {:.5f}, {:.5f}] |\n\n",
             seeded[0][0], seeded[0][1], seeded[0][2], seeded[1][0], seeded[1][1], seeded[1][2]);

  // Every initializer of a [256, 128] weight against its target standard deviation.
  const std::int64_t fin = 256, fout = 128;
  nn::Generator g(7);
  struct Row { std::string name; nn::Initializer f; double target; };
  const double glorot = std::sqrt(2.0 / (fin + fout)), he = std::sqrt(2.0 / fin), lecun = std::sqrt(1.0 / fin);
  const std::vector<Row> rows = {
      {"uniform(0.05)", init::uniform(0.05), 0.05 / std::sqrt(3.0)},
      {"normal(0.02)", init::normal(0.02), 0.02},
      {"truncated_normal(0.02)", init::truncated_normal(0.02), 0.02 * init::truncated_normal_std()},
      {"glorot_uniform()", init::glorot_uniform(), glorot},
      {"glorot_normal()", init::glorot_normal(), glorot},
      {"he_uniform()", init::he_uniform(), he},
      {"he_normal()", init::he_normal(), he},
      {"lecun_normal()", init::lecun_normal(), lecun},
      {"variance_scaling(1, FanIn, TruncatedNormal)", init::variance_scaling(1, init::FanMode::FanIn, init::Distribution::TruncatedNormal), lecun},
      {"variance_scaling(2, FanAvg, Uniform)", init::variance_scaling(2, init::FanMode::FanAvg, init::Distribution::Uniform), std::sqrt(2.0 / 192)},
      {"orthogonal()", init::orthogonal(), 1.0 / std::sqrt(double(fin))},
  };
  std::print("| initializer | target std | sample std / target | sample mean / standard error | largest \\|w\\| / target |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& r : rows) {
    const Tensor t = r.f({fin, fout}, nn::DType::Float64, g);
    const double mean = nn::mean(t).item(), sd = std::sqrt(nn::mean(nn::square(t - mean)).item());
    std::print("| `{}` | {:.5f} | {:.4f} | {:+.2f} | {:.2f} |\n", r.name, r.target, sd / r.target, mean / (r.target / std::sqrt(double(t.numel()))),
               nn::max(nn::abs(t)).item() / r.target);
  }
  const Tensor q = init::orthogonal()({fin, fout}, nn::DType::Float64, g);
  std::print("\nOrthogonal [256, 128]: largest |QᵀQ − I| = {:.1e}. Truncated-normal factor √(1 − 4φ(2)/(2Φ(2) − 1)) = {:.8f}; "
             "gains: tanh {:.4f}, relu {:.4f}, leaky_relu(0.2) {:.4f}, selu {:.2f}.\n",
             nn::max(nn::abs(nn::matmul(nn::transpose(q), q) - nn::eye(fout, nn::DType::Float64))).item(), init::truncated_normal_std(),
             init::gain("tanh"), init::gain("relu"), init::gain("leaky_relu", 0.2), init::gain("selu"));
}
```
