// result_end

\caption(RMS of the pre-activations against layer, on a log scale, for 50-layer networks of width 256. Thin lines: measured. Wide pale lines: the mean-field prediction. Left: ReLU with variance scale s from 1.6 to 2.4 (He is s equals 2). Right: tanh with s equals 1, 25 over 9 and 4.)
\alttext(Left, ReLU: five measured curves fan out from about 0.15 at layer 1. Scale 1.6 falls steadily to about minus 2.5 at layer 50, scale 1.8 to about minus 0.6, scale 2 stays near 0.2 to 0.3, scale 2.2 rises to about 1.2 and scale 2.4 to about 2.4. Each follows its pale straight prediction line, with deviations of up to about 0.4. Right, tanh: scale 1 falls from 0 to about minus 1 along its prediction; scales 25 over 9 and 4 drop within a few layers to flat levels near 0.03 and 0.16, on top of their predictions.)

*ReLU.* The variance scale decides everything, as the recursion says. At \(s=2\) the RMS stays level, ending at 2.07 against a prediction of 1.41. At \(s=1.6\) it falls by a factor
of about 380 over 50 layers, and at \(s=2.4\) it grows by about 150, against predicted factors of 237 and 87. The gradients follow the same law in reverse: the gradient at layer 1 is
0.0044 times that at layer 50 for \(s=1.6\) (predicted 0.0042) and 139 times for \(s=2.4\) (predicted 87). The measured curves wander about their straight-line predictions by up to a factor of
2.6. That is the finite-width effect, since each layer multiplies the squared norm by a random factor whose log has variance of order \(1/n\). Over \(L\) layers these add up to a log-variance proportional to
\(L/n\) (Hanin and Rolnick, 2018), here 50/256.

*tanh.* The mean field is nearly exact. For \(s=1\) (LeCun) the signal decays slowly towards zero, like a power of the depth rather than exponentially, to 0.093 at layer 50 against 0.102 predicted.
For \(s=25/9\) (the conventional gain 5/3) and \(s=4\) it settles within a few layers at the fixed points 1.086 and 1.457, measured 1.08 and 1.43. Yet these
two forward-stable networks have very different gradients. With gain 5/3 the gradient at layer 1 is 83 times that at layer 50, and with \(s=4\) about 1700 times, matching the
predicted \(\prod\chi_l^{1/2}\) of 80 and 1600. Both are in the chaotic phase, \(\chi^\ast>1\). A stable forward signal says nothing about the backward one, and only the gradient factor shows it.

>> The spectrum of a deep linear Jacobian

The next program multiplies \(L\) random \(128\times128\) matrices, Gaussian with variance \(1/128\) or orthogonal, and computes the singular values of the product, pooled
over 10 draws.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/variables_initializers_isometry.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
namespace init = dm::nn::init;
using nn::Tensor;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;
  const std::int64_t n = 128;
  const int draws = 10;
  nn::Generator gen(9);
  // Singular values of the input-output Jacobian J = W_1 W_2 ... W_L of a deep linear network.
  auto spectrum = [&](const nn::Initializer& f, int L) {
    Tensor J = nn::eye(n, F64);
    for (int l = 0; l < L; ++l) J = nn::matmul(J, f({n, n}, F64, gen));
    return nn::svd(J, false).s.to_vector<double>();  // descending
  };
  const auto gauss = init::lecun_normal();  // variance 1/n, so E||Jx||^2 = ||x||^2
  const auto orth  = init::orthogonal();
  std::print("| depth L | mean σ² (theory 1) | mean σ⁴ | theory L + 1 | largest σ | edge √((L+1)^(L+1)/L^L) | median σ | share of σ below 0.01 | orthogonal: largest \\|σ − 1\\| |\n");
  std::print("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> Ls, smax, edge, smed;
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> pooled;
  for (int L : {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32}) {
    double m2 = 0, m4 = 0, top = 0, orth_dev = 0;
    std::vector<double> all;
    for (int d = 0; d < draws; ++d) {
      const auto s = spectrum(gauss, L);
      for (double v : s) m2 += v * v, m4 += v * v * v * v, all.push_back(v);
      top += s.front();
      for (double v : spectrum(orth, L)) orth_dev = std::max(orth_dev, std::abs(v - 1));
    }
    const double count = double(draws * n);
    m2 /= count, m4 /= count, top /= draws;
    std::ranges::sort(all);
    const double e = std::sqrt(std::pow(L + 1.0, L + 1.0) / std::pow(double(L), double(L)));
    const double tiny = double(std::ranges::count_if(all, [](double v) { return v < 0.01; })) / count;
    Ls.push_back(std::log2(L)), smax.push_back(std::log10(top)), edge.push_back(std::log10(e));
    smed.push_back(std::log10(all[all.size() / 2]));
    std::print("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.2f} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.2g} | {:.3f} | {:.1e} |\n", L, m2, m4, L + 1, top, e, all[all.size() / 2], tiny, orth_dev);
    if (L == 4 || L == 16 || L == 32) pooled.push_back(all);
  }

  // Left: density of log10 singular values for L = 4, 16, 32. Right: largest (mean over draws) and median
  // against depth, with the predicted edge; orthogonal stays at 1.
  const dm::graphics::RGB c3[] = {{37, 99, 235}, {234, 88, 12}, {220, 38, 38}};
  const char* names[] = {"L = 4", "L = 16", "L = 32"};
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const double lo = -8, hi = 2, bw = 0.25;
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < pooled.size(); ++k) {
    std::vector<double> centre, density;
    for (double b = lo; b < hi - 1e-9; b += bw) {
      double count = 0;
      for (double v : pooled[k]) { const double lv = std::log10(v); count += lv >= b && lv < b + bw; }
      centre.push_back(b + bw / 2), density.push_back(count / (bw * double(pooled[k].size())));
    }
    left.lines(centre, density, names[k], c3[k], 2.0);
  }
  left.lines({0.0, 0.0}, {0.0, 0.9}, "orthogonal (all σ = 1)", dm::graphics::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 2.0);
  left.x_limits(lo, hi).y_limits(0, 0.9).title("Gaussian weights: spread of singular values").x_label("log10 σ").y_label("density");
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  right.lines(Ls, edge, "predicted largest", dm::graphics::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 5.0);
  right.lines(Ls, smax, "largest", c3[2], 2.0).points(Ls, smax, "", c3[2], 5.0);
  right.lines(Ls, smed, "median", c3[1], 2.0).points(Ls, smed, "", c3[1], 5.0);
  right.lines({0.0, 5.0}, {0.0, 0.0}, "orthogonal", dm::graphics::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 2.0);
  right.x_limits(0, 5).y_limits(-6, 4).title("Singular values against depth").x_label("log2 L").y_label("log10 σ");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(left);
  fig.add(right);
  fig.size(1100, 460).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/variables_initializers_isometry.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| depth L | mean σ² (theory 1) | mean σ⁴ | theory L + 1 | largest σ | edge √((L+1)^(L+1)/L^L) | median σ | share of σ below 0.01 | orthogonal: largest \|σ − 1\| |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 1.002 | 2.02 | 2 | 1.964 | 2.000 | 0.81 | 0.008 | 8.9e-16 |
| 2 | 0.995 | 2.99 | 3 | 2.538 | 2.598 | 0.56 | 0.042 | 1.2e-15 |
| 4 | 0.987 | 5.05 | 5 | 3.361 | 3.494 | 0.27 | 0.146 | 1.1e-15 |
| 8 | 0.971 | 8.37 | 9 | 4.163 | 4.805 | 0.067 | 0.336 | 1.3e-15 |
| 16 | 1.018 | 18.20 | 17 | 5.713 | 6.697 | 0.0037 | 0.558 | 1.8e-15 |
| 32 | 1.002 | 41.12 | 33 | 7.682 | 9.399 | 1.4e-05 | 0.728 | 2.4e-15 |
\image(figures/variables_initializers_isometry.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: the density of log10 singular values of the product of L Gaussian 128 by 128 matrices, for L equals 4, 16 and 32, against orthogonal matrices, whose singular values are all 1 (green). Values below 10 to the minus 8 are not shown. Right: the largest singular value (mean over 10 draws) and the median against depth, with the predicted edge of the spectrum.)
\alttext(Left: for L equals 4, a blue density peaks near log10 sigma equals 0 at about 0.5 and has a long left tail; for L equals 16 and 32, the orange and red densities are low and flat, spread from minus 8 to about 1; all three end just above 0.8 to 1 on the right; a green vertical line at 0 marks the orthogonal case. Right: the largest singular value rises from 0.3 to about 0.88 in log10 as log2 L goes from 0 to 5, just below the grey predicted line; the median falls from about minus 0.1 to about minus 4.8; a green line stays at 0 for orthogonal weights.)

*Gaussian weights.* The mean of \(\sigma^2\) stays within 3% of 1 at every depth, so the network preserves the norm *on average*, as the variance argument promised. The spread grows with depth
as the Fuss–Catalan law predicts. The mean of \(\sigma^4\) is 2.02, 2.99, 5.05, 8.37 and 18.20 for \(L=1\), 2, 4, 8 and 16, against \(L+1\); it is dominated by the few largest values, so with
1280 pooled singular values it scatters by up to 10% about the limit. The largest singular value is within 15% of the
edge \(\sqrt{(L+1)^{L+1}/L^L}\) over the same range, and falls further below it as \(L\) grows. Meanwhile the median falls from 0.81 to 0.0037, and the share of directions shrunk by more than a factor of 100 grows from 0.8% to 56%. At
\(L=32\) the measured spectrum departs from the limit: \(\sigma^4\) averages 41 instead of 33, driven by a few large values, and the largest singular value is 18% below the edge. The law holds as the width grows with the depth fixed,
and here the depth is a quarter of the width. A deep Gaussian network thus passes a few directions through amplified and crushes most of the rest, even though its average norm is right.

*Orthogonal weights.* Every singular value equals 1 to within \(3\times10^{-15}\) at every depth, a perfectly conditioned Jacobian. For linear networks this exact isometry is what
orthogonal initialisation buys. With nonlinearities it holds only approximately, near the fixed point (Pennington et al., 2017).

> A dataset: training a deep network on digits

The digits data (Alpaydin and Kaynak, 1998) give 1797 handwritten digits as \(8\times8\) images with grey levels 0 to 16. Scale the pixels to \([0,1]\), centre them, train on the first
480 (a subset that keeps the run short) and test on the last 360. The network has ten hidden ReLU layers of width 64 and a linear output layer, with no biases. It is trained by 600 steps of plain gradient
descent with learning rate 0.1 on the full training set. Gradient descent, unlike Adam, does not rescale small gradients, so it shows what the initialisation hands it.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/variables_initializers_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
namespace init = dm::nn::init;
using nn::Tensor;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;
  // 1797 digits, 64 pixels scaled to [0, 1] and centred; the first 480 train, the last 360 test.
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::int64_t n = 1797, d = 64, n_train = 480, n_test = 360;
  std::vector<double> v(static_cast<std::size_t>(n * d));
  for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < d; ++j) {
    const auto c = digits.get_column<double>(std::format("pixel_{}", j)).values();
    const double m = std::accumulate(c.begin(), c.end(), 0.0) / n / 16.0;
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) v[i * d + j] = c[i] / 16.0 - m;
  }
  const auto label = digits.get_column<double>("digit").values();
  const Tensor X = nn::from_vector(v, {n, d});
  const Tensor y = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(label, {n}), nn::DType::Int64);
  const Tensor Xtr = nn::slice(X, {0, 0}, {n_train, d}), Xte = nn::slice(X, {n - n_test, 0}, {n_test, d});
  const Tensor ytr = nn::slice(y, {0}, {n_train}), yte = nn::slice(y, {n - n_test}, {n_test});

  // Ten hidden ReLU layers of width 64 and a linear output layer, no biases, trained by plain gradient descent.
  const int hidden = 10, steps = 600;
  const double lr = 0.1;
  struct Run { std::string name; nn::Initializer f; std::vector<double> loss; double test_acc{0}, first_grad{0}, last_grad{0}; };
  std::vector<Run> runs = {{"he_normal", init::he_normal()},
                           {"orthogonal(√2)", init::orthogonal(init::gain("relu"))},
                           {"glorot_normal", init::glorot_normal()},
                           {"normal(0.01)", init::normal(0.01)}};
  for (auto& run : runs) {
    nn::ParameterStore store(5, F64);
    std::vector<Tensor> W;
    for (int l = 0; l <= hidden; ++l) W.push_back(store.get(std::format("W{}", l), {d, l == hidden ? 10 : d}, run.f));
    nn::Optimizer opt(store, lr, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Sgd});
    auto forward = [&](const Tensor& x) {
      Tensor h = x;
      for (int l = 0; l < hidden; ++l) h = nn::relu(nn::matmul(h, W[l]));
      return nn::matmul(h, W[hidden]);
    };
    for (int s = 0; s < steps; ++s) {
      const Tensor loss = nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(forward(Xtr), ytr);
      const auto g = nn::grad({loss}, W);
      if (s == 0) {
        run.first_grad = std::sqrt(nn::mean(nn::square(g.front())).item());
        run.last_grad = std::sqrt(nn::mean(nn::square(g[hidden - 1])).item());
      }
      opt.step(g);
      run.loss.push_back(loss.item());
    }
    nn::NoGradGuard no_grad;
    run.test_acc = nn::mean(nn::cast(nn::equal(nn::argmax(forward(Xte), -1), yte), F64)).item();
  }
  std::print("| initializer | weight std, hidden layer | gradient RMS at step 0: first layer | last hidden layer | loss at step 0 | step 100 | step 200 | step 300 | step 600 | test accuracy |\n");
  std::print("| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  const double stds[] = {std::sqrt(2.0 / d), std::sqrt(2.0 / d), std::sqrt(1.0 / d), 0.01};
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < runs.size(); ++k) {
    const auto& r = runs[k];
    std::print("| `{}` | {:.4f} | {:.2e} | {:.2e} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.1f}% |\n", r.name, stds[k], r.first_grad, r.last_grad, r.loss[0], r.loss[99], r.loss[199], r.loss[299], r.loss.back(), 100 * r.test_acc);
  }
  int left_chance = -1;
  for (int s = 0; s < steps && left_chance < 0; ++s) if (runs[2].loss[s] < 0.9 * std::log(10.0)) left_chance = s + 1;
  std::print("\nln 10 = {:.4f}, the loss of a uniform prediction over the 10 digits. Glorot's loss first falls 10% below it at step {}.\n",
             std::log(10.0), left_chance);

  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const dm::graphics::RGB cols[] = {{37, 99, 235}, {22, 163, 74}, {234, 88, 12}, {220, 38, 38}};
  std::vector<double> step;
  for (int s = 1; s <= steps; ++s) step.push_back(s);
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < runs.size(); ++k) {
    std::vector<double> l;
    for (double x : runs[k].loss) l.push_back(std::log10(std::clamp(x, 1e-4, 100.0)));
    plot.lines(step, l, runs[k].name, cols[k], 2.0);
  }
  plot.lines({0.0, double(steps)}, {std::log10(std::log(10.0)), std::log10(std::log(10.0))}, "ln 10 (chance)", dm::graphics::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.0);
  plot.x_limits(0, steps).y_limits(-4, 4).title("Training loss of a 10-hidden-layer ReLU network on digits")
      .x_label("gradient-descent step (learning rate 0.1)").y_label("log10 cross-entropy");
  plot.size(1100, 460).save("build/variables_initializers_digits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| initializer | weight std, hidden layer | gradient RMS at step 0: first layer | last hidden layer | loss at step 0 | step 100 | step 200 | step 300 | step 600 | test accuracy |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| `he_normal` | 0.1768 | 2.86e-03 | 3.55e-03 | 2.3398 | 0.0198 | 0.0020 | 0.0009 | 0.0003 | 80.3% |
| `orthogonal(√2)` | 0.1768 | 2.96e-03 | 2.97e-03 | 2.3739 | 0.0690 | 0.0036 | 0.0013 | 0.0004 | 88.3% |
| `glorot_normal` | 0.1250 | 1.19e-04 | 9.48e-05 | 2.3019 | 2.2855 | 1.7279 | 0.4810 | 0.0010 | 81.1% |
| `normal(0.01)` | 0.0100 | 9.70e-16 | 7.78e-16 | 2.3026 | 2.3026 | 2.3026 | 2.3026 | 2.3026 | 8.3% |

ln 10 = 2.3026, the loss of a uniform prediction over the 10 digits. Glorot's loss first falls 10% below it at step 161.
\image(figures/variables_initializers_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Training cross-entropy, on a log scale, over 600 gradient-descent steps for four initialisations of the same 10-hidden-layer ReLU network, with the loss of a uniform prediction, ln 10 (grey).)
\alttext(The grey chance line is at log10 of 2.30, about 0.36. The blue He and green orthogonal curves start on it, oscillate with spikes for about 50 steps, then fall smoothly to about minus 3.5 and minus 3.4 by step 600. The orange Glorot curve stays on the chance line until about step 180, then wanders down with repeated spikes back up to the line until about step 340, and finally falls smoothly to about minus 3.0 by step 600. The red curve for normal 0.01 lies on the chance line throughout.)

*Gradient sizes at the start.* He and orthogonal weights (both of variance \(2/64\)) give first-layer gradients of about \(3\times10^{-3}\). Glorot weights have variance \(2/(64+64)=1/64\), the ReLU scale
\(s=1\), which halves the variance at every layer. Their gradients are 24 to 37 times smaller, the same order as the factor \(2^{11/2}\approx45\) for the 11 weight layers.
\(\mathcal N(0,0.01^2)\) weights have \(s=64\times10^{-4}=0.0064\), which divides the variance by about 300 per layer. Their gradients are about \(10^{-15}\), \(10^{12.5}\) times smaller than He's, against a predicted \(10^{13.7}\).

*Training.* With He or orthogonal weights the loss oscillates for about 50 steps, then falls smoothly to below \(10^{-3}\) by step 600. With Glorot weights the loss stays within 10% of
\(\ln10\) until step 161 and is still 0.48 at step 300, because each step is as small as its gradients. Once the weights have grown, it falls in a series of spikes, each a step that overshoots,
and reaches 0.001 by step 600. With \(\mathcal N(0,0.01^2)\) weights the output is essentially zero, the loss is \(\ln10=2.3026\) to four decimals at every checkpoint, and the curve never
leaves the chance line. A learning rate large enough to move such tiny gradients would be catastrophically large once they grew.

The three networks that trained fit their training set almost perfectly without any regularisation, and reach 80% to 88% on the 360 held-out digits after training on only 480. One run each
cannot rank them. The initialisation decided whether and when training started, not where it ended.

> Conclusion

A variable in an eager library is just a tensor that is updated in place. Its initialiser is the part that needs thought. Every standard initialiser draws weights of variance \(s/n\), and the
requirement that each layer pass the signal's variance through unchanged fixes the scale \(s\): 1 for linear layers, 2 for ReLU, with fan-in, fan-out or their average deciding
whether forward signals or backward gradients are protected. The uniform and truncated-normal variants match that variance by the factors \(\sqrt3\) and 0.8796, and the measured
standard deviations of every initialiser in the library agree to within 0.6%.

Depth turns a per-layer factor into a power. The mean-field recursion predicted 50-layer ReLU and tanh networks well: tanh to within 10%, forward and backward, and ReLU to within the factor-of-2 finite-width
fluctuations. It also exposed a trap the forward signal hides. Tanh networks with the conventional gain keep a constant forward signal while their gradients grow 80-fold. Even a
correctly scaled Gaussian network crushes most directions as it deepens, with \(\overline{\sigma^4}=L+1\), while orthogonal weights keep every singular value at 1. On real data the theory
decides whether training starts at all. On digits, the He and orthogonal networks trained at once, the Glorot network waited about 160 steps for its 24-fold smaller gradients to
grow, and the \(\mathcal N(0,0.01^2)\) network, with gradients of \(10^{-15}\), never trained.

